Headers this platform is older than
===================================

Copies of two AOSP headers, taken from the android11-release branch of
platform/hardware/libhardware and unmodified. They shadow the ones this
release ships, because this release ships version 2.3 of the composer
interface and the code above expects 2.4.

    hardware/hwcomposer2.h
    hardware/hwcomposer_defs.h

Why a copy rather than a compatibility header. Everything 2.4 added on the C
side could have been declared alongside the platform's own, as is done for
the display headers in drm/. The C++ half could not: what 2.4 added there are
new members of existing enum classes, and an enum class cannot be added to
from outside. Their code writes `HWC2::Callback::Vsync_2_4`, and the only way
for that to mean anything is for the enumeration to have been declared with
it in the first place.

Why it is safe. Version 2.4 is a superset of 2.3 -- new callbacks, new
functions, new enumeration members, no removals and no changed values, which
is checked rather than assumed:

    HWC2_CALLBACK_VSYNC stays 3, and 2.4 appends 4, 5, 6
    the function descriptors of 2.3 keep their numbers, 2.4 appends from 53
    the error codes of 2.3 keep theirs, 2.4 appends two

So a composer built against 2.4 answers a 2.3 caller exactly as a 2.3 one
would. What the framework on this release never asks for, it never gets --
the entry points are present and unreachable, which is the same arrangement
as the display attributes this hardware has not got.

To retire this directory: build on a release that ships 2.4 or later, drop it
from the include path, and delete it. Nothing outside it refers to it by
name.
